% =====================================================================================
% AUTHOR-LIST POLICY (decided 2026-07-26 from retrieved evidence, not assumption)
% =====================================================================================
% RULE ADOPTED: full author lists everywhere, with exactly ONE exception:
%   armato2011  (56 authors) -- TRUNCATED to "Samuel G. {Armato III} and others", on direct
%                               observed evidence: this reference was found rendered 3 times in
%                               IEEE journals and every one reads "S. G. Armato, III, et al."
%   roberts2021 (54 byline entries) -- FULL. Six observed IEEE Access renderings of this exact
%                               reference all print the complete list, consortium inline.
%   pedrosa2021 (26 authors) -- FULL, for uniformity (researcher's call). No rendering of this
%                               reference exists in the corpus, so there was no observed practice
%                               to follow, and 26 names is close to the 22 IEEE Access is
%                               documented to print in full.
% PRINCIPLE: follow OBSERVED practice where a rendering of that specific reference exists; where
% none exists, prefer the full list. Only armato2011 has observed evidence for truncating, so only
% armato2011 is truncated. A copyeditor may re-normalise any of this at production.
%
% WHY, in order of evidential weight:
% 1. IEEEtran.bst DOES NOT TRUNCATE BY DEFAULT. Read at source (v1.14, CTAN):
%    default.is.forced.et.al = #0, so the truncation branch never fires and the
%    max-names value (#10) is inert. Put 56 names in the .bib and the submitted PDF
%    prints 56 names. Truncation must therefore be done in the .bib or via a
%    IEEEtranBSTCTL entry -- it does not happen by itself. (No at-sign appears anywhere in these
%    notes, on purpose: BibTeX scans for an at-sign to begin an entry and does NOT honour "%" as a
%    comment character, so an at-sign inside a note is parsed as a malformed entry. An earlier
%    version of this very note wrote the character out to explain the rule and produced three
%    BibTeX errors per compile -- visible in output.blg as "You're missing an entry type".)
% 2. WRITTEN IEEE RULE (IEEE Reference Guide V3.28.2025, ieeeauthorcenter.ieee.org):
%    "IEEE publications must list names of all authors, up to six names. If there are
%    more than six names listed, use the primary author's name followed by 'et al.'"
%    Note: FIRST AUTHOR ONLY + et al., not first-six-then-et-al.
% 3. OBSERVED IEEE ACCESS PRACTICE, from a corpus of 17,922 reference entries harvested
%    from 292 IEEE Access articles (publisher-deposited JATS via Europe PMC) plus 283
%    references read off three genuine IEEE-typeset PDFs:
%      - Only TWO forms exist in production: the full list, or ONE name + "et al."
%        There is NO first-N-then-et-al form. Of 274 "et al." references, 269 (98.2%)
%        carry exactly one name; the 5 exceptions sit in two articles whose bibliographies
%        were visibly never copyedited into IEEE style.
%      - The six-name cap is NOT enforced: 2,793 references print >=7 authors in full,
%        the longest observed being 22 names. So a full list in the .bib is printed as-is.
%      - armato2011 specifically was found rendered 3 times, ALWAYS as
%        "S. G. Armato, III, et al." -> we truncate it.
%      - roberts2021 specifically was found rendered 6 times, ALWAYS in full -> we do not.
%    This is why the policy is per-reference rather than a blanket cap.
% ALTERNATIVE, if a global style-driven rule is ever preferred: keep every name in the
% .bib and add an IEEEtranBSTCTL entry with CTLuse_forced_etal="yes", CTLmax_names_forced_etal="6",
% CTLnames_show_etal="1"} with \bstctlcite after \begin{document}. That matches the WRITTEN
% rule exactly but is more aggressive than observed practice -- it would also collapse the
% 7-16-author references that IEEE Access actually printed in full.
% Either way a copyeditor may re-normalise at production; the choice above has the
% advantage that the SUBMITTED pdf already matches the published form.
